<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lex Ripuaria</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">7th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lex Ripuaria is the law code of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ripuarian-franks">Ripuarian Franks</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> region, written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> in the 7th century, probably under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagobert-i">Dagobert I</a>. Based largely on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salic-law">Salic law</a>, it set out compensation payments for injuries and crimes, rules of procedure and property, and the status of different groups. It remained in use under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingians</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The code originated about 630 in the region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> and has been described as a later development of the Frankish laws known from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salic-law">Lex Salica</a>. The compilation apparently goes back to the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagobert-i">Dagobert I</a> (629–639), when the power of the mayors of the palace was still slight: the code threatens a mayor with death for taking bribes in the course of his judicial duties. The first two of its three parts are probably older than the third.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The law has 89 chapters, which fall into three distinct parts. The first part, chapters 1 to 31, is a scale of compositions, the payments owed for offences; the fines are reckoned on a unit of 18 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solidus">solidi</a> rather than the 15 of the Salic Law, but this part already shows Salic influence. Chapters 32 to 64 are taken directly from the Salic Law, in the same order and with the unit of 15 solidi, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitulary">capitularies</a> inserted on the freeing of the unfree and the sale of immovable property. The last part, chapters 65 to 89, gathers provisions of various kinds, some from lost capitularies and the Salic Law, others of unknown origin.</p><p>The Ripuarian law departs further from the old Germanic law than the Salic Law does. Written deeds carry some weight, and the clergy are protected by a higher wergild, 600 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solidus">solidi</a> for a priest and 900 for a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>. More room is given to the cojuratores, or sworn witnesses, and the code records the judicial duel, which the Salic Law does not mention.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The 35 surviving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>, and the lost ones on which the old editions were based, go back no further than the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>. They all give the same text, and it is a revised one, so that only a lex emendata survives. The code was a major influence on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lex-saxonum">Lex Saxonum</a> of 802. R. Sohm edited it for the Monumenta Germaniae, in the fifth volume of its Leges, in 1883.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lex Ripuaria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Ripuarian Franks kept their own law, the <b>Lex Ripuaria</b>, written down in the 7th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-cologne">Kingdom of Cologne</a></li></ul></section>
